violations.llm_prompt:
WHAT: Every api/ trait method that already uses a *Request parameter and *Response success type must have those types declared in dto/<snake_case(TypeName)>.rs, only when this project has opted into the dto/ leaf via allowed_dir_names. The expected file is keyed by the TYPE name, not the method name.

WHY: Request/Response type NAMING alone (api_method_takes_request / api_response_type_named) doesn't guarantee discoverability — a reader has to grep for the type. Filing it under a file named after the type it declares makes the correspondence locatable by path alone. Keying by type rather than method also handles legitimate sharing correctly: some methods intentionally reuse an identical *Request/*Response type (e.g. a shared zero-field UnitRequest marker used by many no-argument accessor methods, or a shared CheckResponse used by a family of boolean-check methods) — method-keyed naming would force duplicating that shared type's declaration once per method, which misrepresents it as method-specific data. Type-keyed naming means every method sharing a type is satisfied by the same single file, with zero special-case logic.

BEFORE (violating, dto/ configured but files missing):
  pub trait UserService {
      fn create_user(&self, req: CreateUserRequest) -> Result<CreateUserResponse, UserError>;  // no dto/create_user_request.rs or dto/create_user_response.rs found
  }

AFTER (compliant):
  // api/dto/create_user_request.rs
  pub struct CreateUserRequest { pub name: String }
  // api/dto/create_user_response.rs
  pub struct CreateUserResponse { pub id: Uuid }

SHARED-TYPE CASE (compliant, no duplication):
  pub trait UserService {
      fn active(&self, req: UnitRequest) -> Result<ActiveResponse, UserError>;
      fn archived(&self, req: UnitRequest) -> Result<ArchivedResponse, UserError>;
  }
  // api/dto/unit_request.rs declares UnitRequest once — satisfies both methods.
  // api/dto/active_response.rs and api/dto/archived_response.rs still each need
  // their own file, since those response types are NOT shared.

EDGE CASES:
- This rule is a no-op (Skip) for any project that has not configured "dto" in allowed_dir_names — it never forces adoption.
- Only methods that already pass api_method_takes_request and api_response_type_named are checked; this rule stays silent on methods that don't yet use the *Request/*Response naming convention at all.
- Theme-grouped layouts (api/<theme>/traits/) expect api/<theme>/dto/, not a flat api/dto/.

